# Facebook & Instagram OAuth Troubleshooting Guide

If you encounter the "Can't load URL" and "Invalid Scopes" errors when connecting Facebook, it means your Meta Developer Console configuration is mismatching what the application is requesting. Both of these issues must be resolved inside the **Meta Developer Portal**.

Follow the steps below to fix these configurations.

---

## 1. Resolving "Can't load URL"
This error occurs because Facebook does not recognize `http://localhost:8000/auth/facebook/callback` as an authorized redirect destination.

### Step 1: Add Valid Redirect URIs
1. Go to the [Meta Developer Console](https://developers.facebook.com/).
2. Select your App.
3. In the left-hand menu, navigate to **Facebook Login** > **Settings** (or under the **Use Cases** > **Facebook Login** configuration).
4. Under **Client OAuth Settings**, locate the **Valid OAuth Redirect URIs** field.
5. Add the following URIs:
   * `http://localhost:8000/auth/facebook/callback` (for Facebook login)
   * `http://localhost:8000/auth/instagram/callback` (for Instagram login)
6. Click **Save Changes**.

### Step 2: Configure App Domains
1. In the left-hand menu of your App dashboard, go to **App Settings** > **Basic**.
2. Locate the **App Domains** field.
3. Type `localhost` or `127.0.0.1`.
4. At the bottom of the page, ensure there is a platform configured. If not, click **Add Platform**, select **Website**, and set the **Site URL** to:
   * `http://localhost:5173` (your frontend app) or `http://localhost:8000` (your backend app).
5. Click **Save Changes**.

---

## 2. Resolving "Invalid Scopes"
This error occurs because the requested scopes (`pages_show_list`, `pages_manage_posts`, `pages_read_engagement`, `instagram_basic`, `instagram_content_publish`) are either not enabled under your app's **Use Cases / Permissions** or your app is restricted by its **App Type**.

### Step 1: Verify App Type
1. Look at the top banner of your App dashboard or go to **App Settings** > **Basic**.
2. If your app is configured as a **"Consumer"** app, Meta will block access to business permissions (`pages_*` and `instagram_*`).
3. **Fix:**
   * If your app is a "Consumer" type, you must create a new app and select the **"Business"** type (or choose "Other" > "Business"). This is required to access page and Instagram publishing APIs.

### Step 2: Add Permissions to Use Cases
Meta now groups permissions into "Use Cases". You must explicitly add these permissions to your app:
1. Go to **Use Cases** in the left sidebar.
2. Locate **Facebook Login** (or **Facebook Login for Business**) and click **Customize** or **Edit**.
3. Under the permissions list, make sure to add/enable:
   * `email`
   * `public_profile`
4. Locate **Pages** (or add it as a new Use Case if not present) and add:
   * `pages_show_list`
   * `pages_manage_posts`
   * `pages_read_engagement`
5. Locate **Instagram Graph API** (or add it) and add:
   * `instagram_basic`
   * `instagram_content_publish`
6. Make sure all these permissions are in **Standard Access** (active for development and testing).

> [!NOTE]
> During development, these permissions will work immediately for your own Meta account (the one registered as the App Admin/Developer). If you want other users to connect their pages, you will need to go through Meta's **App Review** to gain **Advanced Access** before putting the app in "Live" mode.
